Content is stored as WordPress block comments in the `post_content` field. The structure uses three main top-level keys: `module`, `content` (containing `innerContent`), and `builderVersion`. All attributes use a responsive format (`desktop`, `tablet`, `phone`).

**Top-Level JSON Structure:**
```json
{
  "module": {
    "meta": { },
    "decoration": { },
    "advanced": { }
  },
  "content": {
    "innerContent": { }
  },
  "builderVersion": "5.0.0-public-beta.1"
}
```

**Module Configuration:**
*   `module`: Holds configuration for metadata, visual styling, and advanced settings.
    *   `module.meta`: Administrative data (`adminLabel`, `conditions`, `visibility`).
    *   `module.decoration`: Visual CSS styling (e.g., `layout`, `spacing`, `background`, `border`).
    *   `module.advanced`: Inner element styling and advanced options (e.g., `text`, `htmlAttributes`, `css`).
*   `content.innerContent`: The module's HTML content, which must use literal unicode escapes: `\u003c` (`<`), `\u003e` (`>`), `\u0022` (`"`).

**Block Wrapper Syntax:**
*   Leaf (Self-Closing): `<!-- wp:divi/{module} {JSON} /-->`
*   Container (Paired Tags): `<!-- wp:divi/{module} {JSON} -->...<!-- /wp:divi/{module} -->`

The outermost container for all Divi content is the `divi/placeholder` block.

**Responsive Values:**
All value-carrying attributes use a responsive wrapper:
```json
{
  "desktop": {
    "value": "center"
  },
  "tablet": {
    "value": "left"
  },
  "phone": {
    "value": "left"
  }
}
```
*   **Breakpoints:** Divi 5 uses desktop-first (`max-width`) breakpoints. If only `desktop` is specified, that value applies universally.
*   **Nested Values:** For complex values (like padding), the structure nests the object under `value`:
```json
{
  "desktop": {
    "value": {
      "padding": { "top": "60px", "right": "20px", "bottom": "60px", "left": "20px" }
    }
  },
  "tablet": {
    "value": { ... }
  }
}
```

**CSS Mapping and Rendering:**
| CSS Property | JSON Path | Rendered On | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `text-align` | `module.advanced.text.text.desktop.value.orientation` | `.et_pb_text_inner` | Not `!important` |
| `color` | `module.advanced.text.text.desktop.value.color` | `.et_pb_text_inner` | Gets `!important` |
| `max-width` | `module.decoration.sizing.desktop.value.maxWidth` | Root element | Not `!important` |
| `padding` | `module.decoration.spacing.desktop.value.padding` | Root element | Rendered with `!important` |
| `margin` | `module.decoration.spacing.desktop.value.margin` | Root element | Rendered with `!important` |

**Margin Sync Properties:** The margin object supports sync flags:
*   `syncVertical`: `

```json
{
  "module": {
    "meta": {
      "adminLabel": { "desktop": { "value": "Hero Section" } }
    },
    "decoration": {
      "background": {
        "desktop": {
          "value": {
            "color": "#037d87"
          }
        }
      },
      "spacing": {
        "desktop": {
          "value": {
            "padding": { "top": "100px", "bottom": "100px" }
          }
        }
      },
      "sizing": {
        "desktop": {
          "value": {
            "width": "100%"
          }
        }
      },
      "layout": {
        "desktop": {
          "value": {
            "display": "block"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "builderVersion": "5.0.0-public-beta.1"
}
```

!!! warning "Background Images"
    Divi 5 SSR does not reliably render background images set via section JSON attributes. Use a solid `color` in the JSON and inline CSS in a text block for actual background images. See [SSR Rendering — Background Image Gap](../internals/ssr-rendering.md) and [Known Limitations — Rule #4](../playbooks/known-limitations.md).

Modules can reference presets (saved attribute templates) instead of inline values. When a preset is applied, the block JSON includes a preset reference: 🔬 Needs Testing

```json
{
  "module": {
    "meta": {
      "preset": {
        "desktop": { "value": "preset-id-here" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The preset ID maps to a preset definition stored in Divi's preset system. When the preset is updated, all blocks referencing it inherit the changes. See [Presets](../builder/presets.md) for the builder documentation.

Divi 5 supports design variables (global tokens) that can be referenced in block attributes. When a field is connected to a variable, the value appears as a variable reference: 🔬 Needs Testing

```json
{
  "module": {
    "decoration": {
      "spacing": {
        "desktop": {
          "value": {
            "padding": {
              "top": "var(--divi-spacing-section-vertical)",
              "bottom": "var(--divi-spacing-section-vertical)"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

See [Design Variables](../builder/design-variables.md) and [Global Variables](../builder/global-variables.md) for the builder documentation.

Module field values are organized into a nested JSON structure:

```json
{
  "module": {
    "meta": {
      "adminLabel": { "desktop": { "value": "My Info Box" } }
    },
    "decoration": {
      "background": { "desktop": { "value": { "color": "#f5f5f5" } } },
      "spacing": { "desktop": { "value": { "padding": { "top": "20px", "bottom": "20px" } } } }
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "innerContent": { "desktop": { "value": "encoded content here" } }
  },
  "builderVersion": "5.0.0-public-beta.1"
}
```

See [Block Comment Format](../internals/block-format.md) for the complete attribute schema and [Content Encoding](../internals/content-encoding.md) for how HTML content is encoded within the JSON.

Each block carries a JSON configuration object:

```json
{
  "module": {
    "meta": {
      "adminLabel": { "desktop": { "value": "section" } }
    },
    "decoration": {
      "layout": { "desktop": { "value": { "display": "block" } } },
      "background": { "desktop": { "value": { "color": "#037d87" } } },
      "spacing": { "desktop": { "value": { "padding": { "top": "60px", "bottom": "60px" } } } },
      "sizing": { "desktop": { "value": { "width": "80%", "maxWidth": "1080px" } } }
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "innerContent": { "desktop": { "value": "encoded HTML content here" } }
  },
  "builderVersion": "5.0.0-public-beta.1"
}
```

All HTML content placed inside `innerContent` values (e.g., text blocks) must use literal 6-character unicode escape strings, not JavaScript unicode escapes. These sequences are: `<` $\rightarrow$ `\u003c`, `>` $\rightarrow$ `\u003e`, and `"` $\rightarrow$ `\u0022`. When setting these values in JavaScript source code, use double-escaped sequences (e.g., `'\\u003c'`) so they survive JSON stringification and are stored as the literal escape sequence in the database.

Example of encoding:
```json
"content": {
  "innerContent": {
    "desktop": {
      "value": "\\u003ch2\\u003eHeading Text\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eParagraph content here.\\\u003c/p\\u003e"
    }
  }
}
```

When reading the content via REST API, decode the literal escape sequences before processing:
```javascript
function decodeInnerContent(encoded) {
    return encoded
        .replace(/\u003c/g, '<')
        .replace(/\u003e/g, '>')
        .replace(/\u0022/g, '"');
}
```

