The Meta Text options group styles various post metadata elements such as author name, publication date, categories, and comment count. These settings are found in the Design tab and apply to Blog, Portfolio, and Post Title modules. Styling allows control over font, weight, size, color, alignment, letter spacing, line height, text shadow, and font style (italics, capitalization, small caps, underline, strikethrough). 

**Settings:**
- **Meta Font:** Selects the typeface for metadata text.
- **Meta Font Weight:** Adjusts boldness from light to extra-bold.
- **Meta Font Style:** Applies formatting like italics, capitalization, small caps, underline, or strikethrough.
- **Meta Text Alignment:** Positions text (left, center, right, justified).
- **Meta Text Color:** Sets the color using a palette or custom value.
- **Meta Text Size:** Defines font size.
- **Meta Letter Spacing:** Controls character spacing.
- **Meta Line Height:** Sets vertical line distance.
- **Meta Text Shadow:** Adds shadow effect (controls position, blur strength, and color).

**CSS Example:**
```css
/* Style post meta text */
.et_pb_post_meta {
  font-size: 13px;
  color: #999;
  letter-spacing: 0.5px;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}
```

**Number Counter CSS Example:**
```css
/* Style the number counter value */
.et_pb_number_counter .percent p {
  font-size: 48px;
  font-weight: 800;
  color: #0984e3;
}
```
**Percentage Counter CSS Example:**
```css
/* Style the bar counter percentage label */
.et_pb_counter_amount .et_pb_counter_amount_number {
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: #ffffff;
}
```

```css
/* Style the Read More link */
.et_pb_post .more-link {
  font-weight: 700;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1px;
  color: #6c5ce7;
}

.et_pb_post .more-link:hover {
  color: #5a4bd1;
}
```

The Sizing options group manages the physical dimensions and horizontal placement of any Divi 5 element. It is available on all modules, rows, and columns.

**What it controls:** Width, max-width, height, min-height, max-height, and horizontal module alignment.
**Location:** Design Tab → Sizing.
**Responsiveness:** Yes; dimension values can be set independently per breakpoint.

**Settings Reference:**
*   **Width:** Defines the element's horizontal size. Accepts CSS units (px, %, vw, em, rem, vh, etc.).
*   **Max Width:** Sets an upper limit on width, preventing it from exceeding this value.
*   **Min Height:** Establishes a minimum vertical dimension.
*   **Height:** Sets a fixed vertical dimension, overriding content-driven sizing.
*   **Max Height:** Restricts the maximum vertical dimension.
*   **Module Alignment:** Controls horizontal alignment (left/center/right) within the parent. This setting is only available when Width or Max Width is set to less than 100%.

**Code Examples:**
*   To center a module with specific dimensions:
    ```css
    .my-module {
      width: 50%;
      max-width: 600px;
      margin-left: auto;
      margin-right: auto;
    }
    ```
*   To set minimum height for a section:
    ```css
    .hero-module {
      min-height: 80vh;
    }
    ```

The Spacing options group manages the internal and external space around any Divi 5 element, controlling CSS box model properties: margin and padding. It is available on sections, rows, columns, and modules.

**What it controls:** Margin and padding values for all four sides (top, right, bottom, left).
**Location:** Design Tab → Spacing.
**Responsiveness:** Yes; spacing values can be set independently per breakpoint.

**Settings Reference:**
*   **Margin Top/Bottom/Left/Right:** Sets external space surrounding the element.
*   **Padding Top/Bottom/Left/Right:** Sets internal space between content and the element's boundary.
*   **Link Toggle (chainlink icon):** Locks paired directional values (top+bottom or left+right) to maintain symmetry when adjusted.

**Code Examples:**
*   To add padding and remove bottom margin:
    ```css
    .my-module {
      padding: 30px 40px;
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
    ```
*   Using viewport units for responsive spacing:
    ```css
    .my-section {
      padding-top: 8vh;
      padding-bottom: 8vh;
      margin-left: 5vw;
      margin-right: 5vw;
    }
    ```

To set global design tokens using `wp.customize` API: 

1. Navigate to Appearance → Customize (or Divi → Theme Customizer).
2. Execute the following JavaScript code:
```javascript
const api = wp.customize;

// Typography
api('et_divi[body_font]').set('Rubik');
api('et_divi[body_font_weight]').set('400');
api('et_divi[body_font_size]').set('17');
api('et_divi[body_line_height]').set('1.5');

// IMPORTANT: The key is "heading_font_weight", NOT "header_font_weight"
api('et_divi[heading_font_weight]').set('600');
api('et_divi[header_text_size]').set('36');
api('et_divi[header_line_height]').set('1.2');

// Colors
api('et_divi[header_color]').set('#223547');
api('et_divi[font_color]').set('#333333');
api('et_divi[accent_color]').set('#037d87');
api('et_divi[link_color]').set('#037d87');
```
3. Click Publish in the Customizer.

Note: For numeric fields (Body Text Size, Header Text Size, Line Height), direct UI interaction (triple-clicking) may be easier than using the API because the UI might not visually update until you click away.

Use Divi 5's Variable Manager as the primary system for design tokens. Only use Theme Options > Custom CSS for effects that Variable Manager cannot handle (e.g., section numbering, card hover effects, sticky CTAs). Avoid setting the same values in both locations to prevent maintenance confusion.

